          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows a loving emotional state toward St John. The qualification “as a sister” specifies that the love is familial rather than marital or romantic; it does not weaken the expression's clear truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal does not create, renew, or affirm an obligation to marry St John or go to India. Jane expressly denies being bound, and “I love you” explains why she previously would have risked much with him while maintaining the boundary of sisterly affection. Her earlier offer to serve as his assistant is a separate proposed arrangement, which St John rejects unless she becomes his wife.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a deliberate argumentative reply defining Jane's position. Although the exchange is emotionally charged, the avowal is not presented as an involuntary cry, reflex, or discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I replied—\\n\\n“There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the occurrence has a relationally defining and conciliatory function: “as a sister” identifies the only kind of love Jane offers, distinguishing it from the marital relationship St John demands. It therefore affirms personal regard while reinforcing her refusal to become his wife.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is disputing St John's claim that she has promised or engaged herself to missionary work in India. She denies any obligation, explains that her trust and sisterly love for him had made her willing to venture much specifically with him, and maintains that she will not marry him or accept a broader duty to go with strangers.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Jane's sisterly love is presented as genuine personal regard and as the reason for her earlier willingness to accompany St John as an assistant. That proposed assistance is a separate speech act from the focal avowal. St John has rejected an unmarried partnership and treats her as bound to missionary service, while Jane explicitly denies any promise or obligation. His immediate response does not reciprocate or otherwise acknowledge the love; he challenges her fear and continues disputing her decision.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification is confined to the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence clearly establishes the represented avowal's sisterly, non-marital framing and Jane's denial of obligation. It does not independently settle every question about the full history of their relationship or St John's private interpretation of the avowal.\"}"
        }
